<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hirosaki Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1590 CE – 1871 CE · capital Hirosaki</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Tsugaru family at the northern tip of Honshu, founded by a vassal who broke away from the Nanbu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-tamenobu">Oura Tamenobu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanbu-clan">Nanbu</a>, seized the Tsugaru region in the 1570s and 1580s and won recognition directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in 1590, taking the name Tsugaru. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-nobuhira">Nobuhira</a> completed Hirosaki castle in 1611.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The breakaway left lasting enmity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanbu-clan">Nanbu of Morioka</a>. The domain opened new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> land, but its cold climate made harvests unreliable, and the Tenmei famine of the 1780s killed a large share of the population. The fourth lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsugaru-nobumasa">Tsugaru Nobumasa</a>, carried out reforms, and the domain later provided guards for Ezo against Russian incursions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> Hirosaki first joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a> but switched to the imperial side in 1868. After the abolition of domains in 1871 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aomori-prefecture">Aomori Prefecture</a>; Hirosaki castle's keep survives.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hirosaki-domain.jpg" alt="Hirosaki Domain" width="320" height="616"><figcaption>Image: MChew · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsugaru-Nambu_han_in_late_Edo_period.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1590 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hirosaki</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tsugaru Tamenobu, Tsugaru Nobuhira, Tsugaru Nobumasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tsugaru Domain</dd></dl></aside></div>
